The learner inspects each offered entry set, replaces exactly those entries by zero, finds the remaining leading entries, and checks both row echelon conditions. The response is one radio selection among five operations. This is recognition of a successful offered operation, with no comparison to unoffered operations.

Tutorial 3148 (24451a80-db9f-5a0f-a591-41500a69774c) defines the leading entry as the first nonzero entry from the left. Tutorial 3896 (137b0909-9a5e-5edf-8019-24e803307903) requires zero rows at the bottom and successive leading entries strictly to the right. Its staircase image illustrates these two conditions; it introduces no additional operation and is not needed in generated questions. No generated assets are required.

All 15 captured practice questions use ordinary 4 by 4 integer matrices, the shared “Which entries ... could be replaced by 0” wording, five choices, and moderate difficulty. q-42008 (2ab2c24d-7d61-5976-acd1-acab7ad4094a) illustrates two separated erasures; q-42370 (ae036109-4b89-561e-a909-eb01f7ebdfb0) shifts successive leading entries with an existing bottom zero row; q-129423 (3458fbf5-87c4-5bfc-9974-f949a6151b73) creates two bottom zero rows. q-73149 (5da4a360-a207-5df6-9d38-99e7b8b5a765) explicitly offers a21,a31 although a21 is already zero. That operation is well-defined and wrong because a42 remains a conflicting leading entry. Every original bank is retained as a replay profile in source-banks.json; distractor intent beyond the actual failed echelon condition is not inferred.

The canonical example (97237f8e-b317-5c86-86d1-8fd3cc83e66b) erases a32,a42,a43. It has no captured answer bank, so canonical transformation checking is possible but complete bank replay is not. The captured Unicode solution arrow is normalized to TeX. No mathematical source errors were found.

The reasoning stages and solution backbone are: identify the offered successful set, display the original and transformed matrices, verify leading-column order and bottom zero rows, and state that the other offered sets fail. There is no minimum-erasure or necessity argument. Moderate alone is supported; easy and hard have no source evidence. The 4 by 4 size and five-choice response retain the source's visual search workload; restricting new operation sizes to one through three keeps each operation mentally tractable, while the explicit replay/parameter interface accepts any distinct in-range set. Integer magnitude and sign may change because they do not affect which entries are zero. Row arithmetic, pivot normalization, and augmented matrices would change the assessed task and are excluded.

Answer-bearing roles are erased coordinates, surviving leading entries, rows becoming zero, and the unique valid offered operation. Their assignments may change. The existing catalogue supplies many zero structures and is retained as a starting set, not as a global-minimum admission rule. For fresh generation choose a catalogue zero structure, enumerate eligible offered operations of at most three coordinates, choose a successful operation first, then sample compatible numerical values and four failed operations. Choose eligible operation cardinality before choosing coordinates to avoid combinatorial bias toward three entries. Fresh banks use equal-size operations chosen from nearby coordinate sets below and above the correct set in row-major ordering, where available, sampling a direction before a candidate to avoid unequal neighborhood sizes causing an ordering cue. These nearby substitutions are plausible alternatives with shared coordinates; using both available directions prevents unrelated early-row erasures from making the lexicographically largest option a reliable cue. Already-zero coordinates remain eligible in both correct and incorrect operations. Match their count within each fresh bank so identifying no-op coordinates alone cannot choose the key; all five choices still require the same echelon test. This conservative bank extension makes length uninformative and requires examining the actual echelon conditions. Source replay retains the observed mixed-size banks. An operation may include an already-zero coordinate or erase a nonleading entry redundantly. Such cases use the same two echelon conditions and require no optimization. They are conservative extensions of the offered-operation wording; zero-coordinate distractors are directly observed. Source profiles are replayable at original parameters, with every complete bank and key preserved.

Coverage obligations: replay all 15 banks (75 options), test existing and newly created bottom zero rows, pivot shifts and pivot deletion, zero-coordinate distractors, redundant valid operations, multiple possible unoffered successes, varied target coordinates and option positions. Controlled tests hold a matrix fixed while changing its offered successful operation, and hold its bank fixed while changing relevant zero structure to switch the key. Nonzero rescaling must preserve all option validity. Check choice position, option size, coordinate labels and recognizable zero-pattern groups before and after the shared delivery filter. A valid inference from actual leading entries is the assessed mathematics, not an incidental shortcut. Report measured samples without claiming all possible cues or future history-weighted selection are covered.

Repair targets: 056097e0527bd0a8e269 removes the nonzero-only coordinate admission rule; 0cd20f2062880b12cb13 removes global minimization from admission, sampling, prompt, and solution. The retained exact counterexamples will run before and after repair. Inventory inspection is read-only; captured non-source prompts with optimization wording are observed, but absent historical generation metadata and unreproduced stored identities prevent certified affected-record claims. Original source questions must never be relabeled as generated.

